{"name":"Acacia Wood","hex":"#d85b45","fetched":"2026-10-28","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"acacia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub or tree of the tribe Acacieae. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The thickened or dried juice of several species in Acacieae, in particular Vachellia nilotica (syn. Acacia nilotica, Egyptian acacia)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A roll or bag, filled with dust, borne by Byzantine emperors, as a memento of mortality. It is represented on medals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/acacia"},{"term":"wood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The substance making up the central part of the trunk and branches of a tree. Used as a material for construction, to manufacture various items, etc. or as fuel.","The wood from a particular species of tree."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or plant with trees.","To hide behind trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wood"}],"entries":[{"title":"Acacia","blocks":[{"text":"Acacia, commonly known as wattles or acacias, is a genus of about 1,084 species of shrubs and trees in the subfamily Mimosoideae of the pea family Fabaceae. Initially, it comprised a group of plant species native to Africa, South America, and Australasia, but is now reserved for species mainly from Australia, with others from New Guinea, Southeast Asia, and the Indian Ocean."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG/330px-Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acacia","wikidata":"Q81666"},{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a natural, lignocellulosic material derived primarily from the secondary xylem of trees and other woody plants, where it provides mechanical support and in the functional sapwood, conducts water and dissolved minerals. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood","wikidata":"Q287"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG/500px-Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG/1280px-Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG/500px-Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/500px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/1280px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/500px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bipinnate leaves of Acacia dealbata","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Acacia_penninervis_%285368395701